  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>An <strong>aneurysm</strong> is a local bulge in an artery wall, like a swelling on a garden hose under pressure. Aneurysms most often form where large arteries branch at the base of the brain: the anterior communicating, middle cerebral and internal carotid arteries.</p>
    <p>The danger is that the thinned wall can <strong>rupture</strong>. Blood spills under the brain coverings — a subarachnoid haemorrhage, a severe form of haemorrhagic stroke. The first day after rupture is especially dangerous: the aneurysm can bleed again, and each rebleed is more severe.</p>
    <h3>Risk factors</h3>
    <p>Aneurysms or brain haemorrhages in close relatives, inherited connective tissue disorders, polycystic kidney disease, as well as smoking and high blood pressure.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <p>Most aneurysms <strong>cause no symptoms</strong> before rupture — they are found by chance on an MRI done for another reason. Warning signs may be:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A sentinel bleed</strong> — a sudden, very severe headache, like a blow to the head, with nausea, vomiting and sensitivity to light. It may pass, but it is a warning.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve compression by a large aneurysm</strong> — double vision, a drooping eyelid, a dilated pupil on one side, pain behind the eye, numbness of half of the face.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={`${tg}/220`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">More about warning signs</a> is on my Telegram channel (in Russian).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>MR or CT angiography</strong> — shows whether there is an aneurysm, its size and shape.</li>
      <li><strong>Cerebral angiography</strong> — the most accurate study of brain vessels; done to plan treatment.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you have risk factors, the route starts with a neurologist: they assess symptoms and family history and decide whether vessel imaging is needed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Observation</strong> — for small aneurysms with a low risk of rupture: follow-up imaging, blood pressure control, stopping smoking.</li>
      <li><strong>Microsurgical clipping</strong> — open surgery: the aneurysm is closed from outside with a titanium clip.</li>
      <li><strong>Endovascular treatment</strong> — the aneurysm is filled from inside with coils through a catheter, sometimes with a stent.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The strategy is chosen by the size, shape and location of the aneurysm and the patient's age and condition. If an aneurysm is dangerous, it is better treated as a planned operation — before rupture, when the outlook is much better.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>How clipping is done</h2>
    <p>The operation is done under general anaesthesia. Through a small approach in the fronto-temporal area, behind the hairline, under the microscope:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>the parent arteries and the aneurysm neck are dissected;</li>
      <li>if needed, blood flow is temporarily stopped to work on the aneurysm safely;</li>
      <li>a permanent titanium clip is placed on the neck — the aneurysm is excluded from the circulation;</li>
      <li>fluorescence angiography during surgery confirms that the aneurysm is excluded and nearby vessels are open;</li>
      <li>the bone is put back.</li>
    </ol>
